  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 215 PM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nA weak 500-mb trough looks to be located over portions of Idaho, \nMontana, and Wyoming Saturday morning. This feature is expected to \nbe moving slowly eastward throughout the long term period. A weak to \nmodest jet streak may develop over Kansas during the afternoon hours \nahead of this feature, which would support a surface low pressure \nover the area and back into the South-Central Plains. A cold front \nwould seem likely as this surface low passes through the area, which \nwould cool temperatures down a bit and provide some chances for \nprecipitation. High temperatures are currently forecast in the upper \n70s and lower 80s Saturday. This is supported by LREF standard \ndeviations in wind direction which show the highest uncertainty in \nwind direction south and east of the forecast area by noon Saturday. \nThis would suggest the front would have already come through the \narea by that time. However, NBM 75th and higher percentiles in high \ntemperature for Saturday show highs in the mid 80s to lower 90s, \nwhich could indicate a slower frontal passage. Currently, the faster \nfrontal passage is favored, with about 75% of LREF members \nsuggesting highs lower than the mid 80s. \n\nNBM guidance also shows most of the forecast area at a 40-50% chance \nof receiving 0.1 inches of rain or higher Saturday, the exception \nbeing areas north of US Highway 24 and east of Kansas Highway 25. \nHowever, LREF guidance shows less than a 25% chance across the \nforecast area that surface CAPE (a measure of atmospheric \ninstability) will be above 1000 J/kg. As such, widespread rainfall \nwith perhaps a few localized showers are favored as opposed to \nconvective thunderstorms. \n\nSurface winds are forecast to be weak, but out of the north Sunday \nas the upper level trough moves in overhead. Conditions look to be \nrelatively temperate, with highs forecast in the low to mid 70s, and \nchances for precipitation decreasing. NBM guidance shows less than a \n25% chance of receiving greater than 0.1 inches of rain Sunday \nexcept for areas along and west of Kansas Highway 27 and south of I-\n70. Any activity is once again expected to be just rainfall and \nperhaps some localized showers instead of thunderstorms, as LREF \nguidance continues to show lower values of CAPE. \n\nForecast guidance becomes more uncertain by Monday morning. \nSpecifically, ensembles show the 500-mb trough getting muted beyond \nMonday, or spread increasing around the feature. When looking at \ndeterministic guidance, this is in relation to how quickly the \ntrough will move out of the area and into the Midwest. However, \nensemble guidance is in good agreement that troughing will redevelop \nacross the Western United States Monday and Tuesday. Taking this \ninto account, a reasonable forecast would be conditions remaining \nlike Sunday until the initial trough moves out of the area. Once \nthat happens, temperatures would begin to increase ahead of the new \ntrough to the west, along with elevated chances for\nprecipitation.",
